00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.500
Christian rapper Flame sued Katy Perry,

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.890
claiming that this song

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:16.890
(music of "Dark Horse" by Katy
Perry feat. Juicy J plays)

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.392
copied this song.

00:00:20.392 --> 00:00:25.142
(music of "Joyful Noise" by Flame plays)

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.737
A jury agreed and awarded
him 2.8 million dollars.

00:00:30.737 --> 00:00:31.990
(register cashing)

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.373
But when a jury found that the writers

00:00:33.373 --> 00:00:36.653
of Katy Perry's 2013 hit
"Dark Horse" featuring Juicy J

00:00:36.653 --> 00:00:40.230
had copied part of a 2008
song called "Joyful Noise"

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.517
by Christian rapper Flame,
it set off a major firework

00:00:43.517 --> 00:00:45.040
in the music industry.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.840
The jury ordered Perry and
the other writers to pay

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.230
almost 2.8 million dollars
to Flame for copying

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.610
a small part of his song.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.830
However, after Perry's
lawyers filed a motion

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.060
to overturn the verdict,
the trial court decided

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.500
it was time to hear it roar.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.480
See what I did there?

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.570
In March, the court threw
out the jury's verdict,

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.300
dismissing the case,
and believe it or not,

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.460
Katy Perry was saved by none
other than Led Zeppelin.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.060
I am not kidding, that is
actually what happened.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.690
To understand how this case
would up being so controversial,

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.160
we have to take a dive
into federal copyright law

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.687
and music theory, so strap in.

00:01:16.687 --> 00:01:19.354
(rousing music)

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.640
Hey Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.980
because I'm just a California girl, no.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.446
No, no more Katy Perry
references, I'm sorry.

00:01:28.446 --> 00:01:30.500
The dispute over "Dark Horse"

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.110
is not about the songs themselves,

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.010
but about a short eight note
melody that Flame believes

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.500
the "Dark Horse" writers copied

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.310
from his song "Joyful Noise".

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.390
And it's true, they both have
a similar musical component

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.140
called an ostinato, and that's
actually sort of unusual

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.530
for a copyright case.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.350
See, usually in lawsuits like this,

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.100
you're comparing the entire song.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.720
For example, George Harrison
was successfully sued

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.910
by The Chiffons over his
song "My Sweet Lord",

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:56.620
which was suspiciously similar

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.461
to The Chiffons' "He's So Fine".

00:01:58.461 --> 00:02:03.378
("My Sweet Lord" by George Harrison plays)

00:02:06.902 --> 00:02:11.485
("He's So Fine" by The Chiffons plays)

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:14.903
Here, the songs are pretty different

00:02:14.903 --> 00:02:17.910
but the songs at issue do
have a similar component,

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.050
their ostinatos.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.250
An ostinato is a short melody
or pattern, like an arpeggio,

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.920
that is constantly repeated
throughout the song,

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.190
usually in the same
part at the same pitch.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.550
However, proving copyright
infringement in music is complex,

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.800
because there are only a
limited number of notes

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.010
and arrangements available
to composers and musicians.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:36.840
If you listen to the radio
for 30 minutes today,

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.910
for example, you will hear common themes

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.780
repeated in many songs.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.700
And it's for this reason
that the individual elements

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.870
of a song simply are not
protected by copyright,

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.360
and that's why music industry
professionals were alarmed

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.940
by the Perry verdict
for 2.8 million dollars.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.470
Many songs use the same
musical building blocks.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.540
If one musician can copyright
the underlying structure,

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.850
it would radically change
the musical landscape

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.860
and allow for musical monopolies.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.820
But I'm not an expert in music theory.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.860
If only I had some expert I could turn to

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.593
to explain the musical concepts at issue.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.310
Wait a minute, I've got an idea.

00:03:13.078 --> 00:03:14.768
(amp clicking on)

00:03:14.768 --> 00:03:18.185
(headphones plugging in)

00:03:23.097 --> 00:03:25.757
(bass riff playing)

00:03:25.757 --> 00:03:28.590
(whooshing music)

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.640
- Well it appears that
I have been summoned.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.330
So "Joyful Noise" and "Dark Horse"

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.470
both employ similar ostinatos.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.350
An ostinato is a simple
repeated melodic fragment

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.510
on top of which a song writer
or composer might write

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.660
more prominent melodies.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.220
So it's kinda like the
backbone of a composition.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.210
Both songs' ostinatos are very similar,

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.470
as the defense pointed out.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.371
They both are in minor keys,
they both have the same rhythm

00:03:52.371 --> 00:03:55.030
of eight evenly spaced quarter notes.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.530
They both start on the
third note or scale degree

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.300
of the minor scale, repeat
that note four times,

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.380
then descend down to the
second note of the minor scale

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:04.790
and repeat that note twice.

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:06.600
After this, "Dark
Horse"'s ostinato descends

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:07.590
to the first scale degree,

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.410
and then below to the fifth scale degree,

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:11.480
and "Joyful Noise"'s
ostinato actually changes

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:12.400
every other time.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.600
The first time it resolves
to the first scale degree,

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:16.020
and the second time, you hear it resolve

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.712
down to the sixth scale degree.

00:04:17.712 --> 00:04:22.712
(plays ostinato of
"Joyful Noise" on piano)

00:04:24.705 --> 00:04:29.538
(plays ostinato of "Dark Horse" on piano)

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.940
So technically, they're different,
but they are very similar

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.940
in the fact that they both are ostinatos,

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:39.270
they're in minor keys,
they have the same rhythm,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.870
and they both start the same way

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.260
with the same scale degrees.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.440
Now the problem that I have
with this is that these elements

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:48.330
aren't remotely unique to
"Joyful Noise" or "Dark Horse".

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.190
You can find any combination
of these elements

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.930
in our shared musical
tradition quite easily.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.210
For example, eight quarter note ostinatos,

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:55.930
like Pachabel's "Canon in D".

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.850
Or for example, minor key ostinatos

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.410
that start on the third
degree and descend down

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.660
to the first degree, like
"Carol of the Bells",

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.560
or of course, the bass line
to "Fancy" by Iggy Azalea.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.807
Or you could specific,
like eight note melodies

00:05:06.807 --> 00:05:10.530
in minor keys that start
on the third scale degree,

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.610
repeat four times and then descend down

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.255
to the second scale degree
and then repeat again,

00:05:15.255 --> 00:05:18.560
you can find that in Bach's
"Violin Sonata in F minor",

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:19.574
the adagio movement.

00:05:19.574 --> 00:05:23.145
(plays adagio movement of
"Violin Sonata in F minor")

00:05:23.145 --> 00:05:26.624
(plays ostinato of "Joyful Noise")

00:05:26.624 --> 00:05:30.177
(plays ostinato of "Dark Horse")

00:05:30.177 --> 00:05:31.070
Now taken individually,

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.480
these points of similarity are banal.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.780
They represent really fundamental
building blocks of music,

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.300
even that specific sequence
of notes, of a third degree

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.518
going to the second degree.

00:05:40.518 --> 00:05:42.130
- Thanks Adam, really appreciate it,

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:43.370
and I promise only to summon you

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:44.710
if it's a musical emergency.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.190
Definitely check out Adam's channel

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.850
if you have any interest in music theory

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:49.890
or bass guitar in general, it's amazing.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.360
And as Adam points out,
the eight note ostinato

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.690
in "Dark Horse" sounds
a lot like the ostinato

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:58.190
in "Joyful Noise", but that's
not the end of the analysis.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:59.550
There are lots of different ostinatos

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.140
that sound similar to that.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.700
Just because something sounds similar

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:03.780
doesn't necessarily mean

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:05.410
that there was copyright infringement.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:06.810
The ostinatos at issue sound

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.170
like a whole bunch of other songs.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.670
Are all of these other songs
infringing on each other?

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.010
To understand the answer to that question,

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:14.540
we have to take a deeper dive
into the songs themselves

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.920
and into copyright law.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.970
To prove a claim of
copyright infringement,

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:20.190
the copyright owner has
to prove two elements.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.460
First, the person has
to own a valid copyright

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:24.990
and two, they have to show
that the protectable portions

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.160
of the copyrighted work
have been infringed,

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:30.220
and infringement is usually
defined as unlawful copying.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.850
Now while that sounds like a clear test,

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.130
it's really just the tip of the iceberg.

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.385
To prove unlawful copying,
you have to show that a person

00:06:36.385 --> 00:06:39.160
actually copied the work at issue,

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.097
which usually requires proving
access, and it also requires

00:06:42.097 --> 00:06:44.210
proving substantial similarity,

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:46.435
which is that the things that were copied

00:06:46.435 --> 00:06:49.818
give rise to the standard that we say

00:06:49.818 --> 00:06:52.040
that it was enough copying

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.700
to constitute copyright infringement.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.600
Now, access and actual
copying are really important

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.120
because technically, independent
creation is a defense

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.100
to copyright infringement.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.910
If you happen to come up with
the exact same form of art,

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:08.790
technically if you, say,
were on a uninhabited island

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.380
in the South Pacific when
you created this thing,

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:13.120
well then there was no way for you

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.530
to have copied someone else.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:17.950
So not only does something have
to be substantially similar

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.766
in terms of what is created,
but you also have to prove

00:07:20.766 --> 00:07:23.560
that someone had access to
the thing that was copied

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:26.380
and that they actually
did do the copying itself.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.450
In most copyright cases, direct
evidence isn't available,

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:32.020
so that's why creators rarely
have proof that the defendant

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:35.420
actually listened to a song or
directly pilfered parts of it

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:36.350
for their own use.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:39.150
Instead, plaintiffs rely often
on circumstantial evidence

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.060
to make their case.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:44.170
Now that's just to prove that
copying of some form existed.

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:46.810
But what about the test
for substantial similarity

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.647
to prove that the things that were copied

00:07:48.647 --> 00:07:50.950
were copied in a way that gives rise

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:51.990
to copyright infringement?

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:53.750
Well, in determining whether two works

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:55.620
are substantially similar, the courts use

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.690
a two part analysis: an
objective extrinsic test

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:00.910
and a subjective intrinsic test.

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:03.409
Now, the subjective intrinsic
test is almost always

00:08:03.409 --> 00:08:05.464
a question for the jury,
it's something a court

00:08:05.464 --> 00:08:07.380
can't decide itself.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:10.700
It asks would a normal
person with no background

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:14.864
in musicology or in whatever
kind of art in an issue here,

00:08:14.864 --> 00:08:19.310
would they feel that the
two works are similar enough

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.910
that copying is actionable?

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.680
But the extrinsic test
considers whether two works

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.820
share a similarity of ideas and expression

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.880
as measured by external
objective criteria.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.750
The extrinsic test requires
analytical dissection of a work

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:35.320
and expert testimony,
and that dissection means

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:36.650
that the court will break down the works

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.130
into their constituent parts
and compare their elements.

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:40.717
This is an analysis that the court,

00:08:40.717 --> 00:08:43.740
the judge in this particular case, can do.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.110
It's objective, it's based on the face

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:47.110
of the works themselves.

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.149
And when the court conducts this analysis,

00:08:49.149 --> 00:08:52.250
it's looking only at
the protected elements

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:53.790
of a copyrighted work.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.370
It's always essential to distinguish

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:57.540
between the protected
and unprotected material.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.028
For example, if something
is in the public domain,

00:09:00.028 --> 00:09:03.480
you can use it and you
can make a new work,

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:06.520
but you only get protection
over the new protected elements,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.260
not the unprotected things
that are in the public domain.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.640
And this is where Flame ran
into trouble with the court.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.150
He was unable to show that
that one brief section

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:16.944
of "Joyful Noise" was
sufficiently original

00:09:16.944 --> 00:09:20.170
in its protectable copyrightable parts,

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:23.250
and not every part of Flame's
song was copyrightable.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.655
For a creative work to be
entitled to copyright protection,

00:09:25.655 --> 00:09:28.040
it must be an original work of authorship,

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:30.560
and when the Supreme
Court defined originality,

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.040
it set a pretty low bar.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.460
A work is original if it
is independently created,

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.370
possessing at least some
minimal degree of creativity.

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.460
And as I mentioned before, this
is a really interesting case

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:41.980
because usually you're
dealing with the whole song

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:44.520
or the whole book or the whole movie.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.440
Here, we're not talking about
both songs in their entirety

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:52.050
but merely constituent parts,
and some musical elements

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.390
are not protectable at all,
such as a chord progression

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:57.670
or a recurring vocal phrase
or a syncopated rhythm,

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:00.120
to name just a few
common elements of songs.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.687
As one court remarked,
"These are building blocks

00:10:02.687 --> 00:10:04.127
"which belong to the public domain

00:10:04.127 --> 00:10:06.910
"and cannot be appropriated
by any particular author."

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:09.710
Because in Western music,
there's only so much stuff

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:11.610
you can do within the key of the song.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:14.586
Musical emergency.

00:10:14.586 --> 00:10:17.179
(plays bass riff)

00:10:17.179 --> 00:10:18.590
(whooshing music)

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.250
- You see, in Western
music, we have this thing

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.320
called tonality, where all
of the notes of the scale

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.310
have a specific function to play.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:27.230
They want to do certain things.

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:29.272
So if you're writing
a song using tonality,

00:10:29.272 --> 00:10:32.349
which chances are, if
you're writing a pop song,

00:10:32.349 --> 00:10:35.660
it's gonna be tonal, there are
a very limited number of ways

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.050
each note can plausibly
resolve to the next

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:39.970
in a pleasing manner.

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:41.410
And if you're writing a pop song,

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:43.810
you want it to be pleasing in theory.

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:46.989
So while yeah, the two
ostinatos are similar,

00:10:46.989 --> 00:10:49.598
musically, that doesn't mean a whole lot.

00:10:49.598 --> 00:10:51.890
- Yeah, and what's really interesting here

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:54.160
is that we're dealing
with a small sub-component

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:55.510
of a song, the ostinato.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:56.960
By way of analogy, imagine you have

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.780
a copyright infringement
case with two novels.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:02.570
The whole work itself would be the novel.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:05.200
That novel is broken
up into sub-components,

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.098
either sentences or words,
and those words themselves

00:11:09.098 --> 00:11:11.670
are broken up into letters.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:13.810
I'm not aware of any
particular copyright case

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.420
that allows you to copyright
an individual word,

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:17.650
it's just too small.

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:20.130
But you can string a whole
bunch of letters together

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:22.350
to form words, and string a
whole bunch of words together

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:24.660
to form an entire novel,
and at some point,

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.720
it has the creativity
necessary to be copyrightable.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:29.621
And a song is the same way.

00:11:29.621 --> 00:11:31.930
If you start with the whole song,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:34.220
that is made up of individual components,

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:37.272
often musical phrases or
maybe it's a drum line

00:11:37.272 --> 00:11:40.830
versus the melody and the
vocal melody and the words,

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:44.720
and those things are made up
often of individual notes,

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:48.130
just like letters make
up an individual word.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:51.720
And I suppose it could
be theoretically possible

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:55.670
to have a very small
number of notes to form

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:58.270
a copyrightable work, but
you would imagine it would be

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:02.550
very very difficult to have
an eight note musical phrase

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:05.210
become a copyrightable thing itself.

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:07.260
And in fact, the 9th Circuit has found

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:10.604
that a four note melody
is not copyrightable.

00:12:10.604 --> 00:12:12.620
Here, we're dealing with eight notes.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:13.790
And obviously there's a spectrum.

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:16.350
At some point, you go into
the hundreds or thousands

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:18.780
of notes, and that becomes
an entire song itself.

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:21.520
And here, although the jury
heard extensive testimony

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.420
about the similarities
between "Joyful Noise"

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:25.705
and "Dark Horse", the
plaintiff simply couldn't prove

00:12:25.705 --> 00:12:29.910
that the eight note
ostinato was original enough

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:31.947
to receive copyright protection.

00:12:31.947 --> 00:12:34.817
"Expressions that are
standard, stock or common

00:12:34.817 --> 00:12:36.877
"to a particular subject matter or medium

00:12:36.877 --> 00:12:39.400
"are not protectable under copyright law"

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:41.150
is what the judge found.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:43.810
Now, the plaintiff's expert
musicologist identified

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:46.280
five distinct elements that
he thought were protectable,

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:48.670
as they were used by "Joyful Noise".

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:50.330
In the plaintiff's brief, they identified

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:52.650
even more elements, a total
of nine, that they thought

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:54.220
deserved copyright protection.

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:56.440
And in response, Katy Perry
and the other defendants

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:58.410
argued that it didn't matter
whether there were three

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.160
or five or nine elements,
since none of them

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:01.590
were protectable on their own.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:04.460
And in a way before Adam
Neely, the court also referred

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.430
to other songs that featured
very very similar ostinatos,

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:10.073
like "Merrily We Roll Along"
and "Jolly Ole Saint Nicholas"

00:13:10.073 --> 00:13:12.310
to explain that the particular ostinato

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:13.530
was not copyrightable.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:15.240
But the court didn't stop there

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:16.640
because there are circumstances

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:18.840
where you can string together a series

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:20.870
of unprotectable elements to form

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:22.980
a larger protectable thing.

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:24.970
I mean, that is what a novel is.

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:27.210
Every individual word
is not copyrightable,

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.020
but if you string enough of them together,

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:31.470
then that does eventually
become copyrightable.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.340
So in theory, Flame could
have proven infringement

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:36.940
of the ostinato if he was able
to show that the combination

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.311
of unprotectable elements
are original enough

00:13:39.311 --> 00:13:41.990
in the way that they are arranged.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:44.870
And you can combine
non-copyrightable things together

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:46.460
to get protection, but when you do that,

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:49.624
you generally get what's called
thin copyright protection.

00:13:49.624 --> 00:13:52.570
If the underlying things
themselves are not copyrightable,

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:54.520
then the only thing that
you get copyright over

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:57.810
is the actual arrangement itself.

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:00.960
And when you are dealing with
thin copyright protection

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:03.660
and you claim that someone
has infringed the copyright

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:06.990
on your selection, then
generally those two things

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:08.520
have to be virtually identical,

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.270
or there is no copyright infringement.

00:14:10.270 --> 00:14:13.377
And as the court found, "It
is undisputed in this case,

00:14:13.377 --> 00:14:15.217
"even viewing the evidence
in the light most favorable

00:14:15.217 --> 00:14:17.027
"to the plaintiffs, that
the signature elements

00:14:17.027 --> 00:14:19.447
"of the eight-note
ostinato in 'Joyful Noise'

00:14:19.447 --> 00:14:22.527
"is not a particularly
unique or rare combination

00:14:22.527 --> 00:14:25.037
"even in its deployment as an ostinato.

00:14:25.037 --> 00:14:26.937
"Prior compositions, including prior works

00:14:26.937 --> 00:14:29.727
"composed by the parties,
as well as what all agree

00:14:29.727 --> 00:14:31.757
"is a separate non-infringing ostinato

00:14:31.757 --> 00:14:35.020
"in 'Dark Horse', all
contain similar elements."

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:37.650
Since the sole musical
phrase that Flame claimed

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:40.400
was infringement was not
a protectable expression,

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.630
the extrinsic test was not satisfied

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:46.223
and therefore Flame's claim
failed as a matter of law.

00:14:46.223 --> 00:14:47.870
Whew, that was hard to say.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:49.850
Now, it's theoretically
possible to copyright

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:51.770
a musical phrase, but it would need to be

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:53.073
really really special.

00:14:53.073 --> 00:14:55.680
It is, as it should be,
really hard to copyright

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.870
sub-components of songs,
because lots of people

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:01.380
use the exact same thing
over and over again.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:05.440
We have a musical library that
is inherent to Western music.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.290
Imagine if someone tried to
copyright this drum rhythm.

00:15:08.290 --> 00:15:11.500
(drum rhythm plays)

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:14.296
Or if someone tried to
copyright this lick.

00:15:14.296 --> 00:15:19.296
(musical lick plays on
various instruments)

00:15:21.533 --> 00:15:23.210
(whooshing music)

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:24.870
Sorry man, false alarm.

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:25.703
Sorry about that.

00:15:25.703 --> 00:15:28.530
Now, Flame's expert musicologist
pointed to nine elements

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:31.401
that he claimed, when taken
together, made an otherwise

00:15:31.401 --> 00:15:34.430
non-copyrightable set
of notes copyrightable.

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:37.150
He pointed to a melody
built in the minor mode,

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:39.620
a phrase length of eight
notes, a pitch sequence

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:42.360
beginning with three, three,
three, three, two, two,

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:44.930
a similar resolution to both phrases,

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:47.550
a rhythm of eighth notes,
a square and even rhythm,

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.310
the structural use of the
phrase as an ostinato,

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:51.930
the timbre of the instrumentation

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:54.400
and the notably empty and sparse texture

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:55.470
of the compositions.

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.010
The thing is, none of
these things by themselves

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:01.220
are copyrightable, and even when you use

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:03.810
all of them together, it doesn't turn

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:05.230
an otherwise non-copyrightable thing

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:07.100
into a copyrightable phrase.

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:08.990
If the sheet music isn't copyrightable,

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:10.860
you can't get protection by playing

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:13.280
that same thing on a synthesizer.

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.190
It's theoretically
possible to get a copyright

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:17.240
over the selection of
unprotected elements,

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:20.660
but you have to show a ton of creativity

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.810
and more than what Flame has alleged here.

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:25.549
It would be much easier
to do if he was claiming

00:16:25.549 --> 00:16:28.830
that the entire song
violated the copyright,

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:31.800
rather than just this really small part

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.130
that is otherwise similar.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:35.470
Now, the court went on to
note that even if the ostinato

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:39.190
was creative enough to
be considered protectable

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:41.956
under copyright, it wasn't
substantially similar

00:16:41.956 --> 00:16:44.030
to the "Dark Horse" ostinato.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.640
In other words, there
was no actionable copying

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:48.280
or copyright infringement.

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:50.900
A selection of a song is only
infringed if the two works

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.030
substantially share the same combination

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:54.610
of protectable elements.

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.500
And remember, the actual
notes themselves in this case

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:58.560
were not protectable.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:02.660
And this is where Led
Zeppelin saved Katy Perry.

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:04.370
Yes, seriously.

00:17:04.370 --> 00:17:06.133
- Does anybody remember laughter?

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.010
- See, in this case,
the protectable elements

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:10.870
would be so narrow that a combination

00:17:10.870 --> 00:17:12.940
of protectable elements
between the two songs

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:16.040
would need to be virtually
identical, and the court held

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:18.530
that Flame was unable to satisfy this test

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:20.020
for substantial similarity.

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:22.270
In making this decision,
the judge referred

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:24.290
to another huge copyright case

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.050
that the 9th Circuit
Court of Appeals decided

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:27.540
just a couple of weeks ago.

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:30.500
There, the 9th Circuit
upheld the 2016 decision

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:33.168
that Led Zeppelin's "Stairway
to Heaven" did not infringe

00:17:33.168 --> 00:17:37.300
on the band Spirit's 1968
instrumental track, "Taurus".

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:41.760
Now, these two songs are much much closer

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.890
than "Dark Horse" and "Joyful Noise".

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:46.270
Here's Led Zeppelin's
unbelievably famous intro

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:47.220
to "Stairway to Heaven".

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:51.720
(intro of "Stairway to Heaven" plays)

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:03.006
And here's Taurus' song "Spirit".

00:18:03.006 --> 00:18:06.506
(music of "Taurus" plays)

00:18:14.054 --> 00:18:17.730
Now, to my ear, those songs,
and here we're talking

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:19.320
about the whole songs in general,

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:21.550
sound much much more similar

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:24.660
than "Dark Horse" does to "Joyful Noise".

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:26.070
And the 9th Circuit agreed.

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:28.627
It held, "We have never
extended copyright protection

00:18:28.627 --> 00:18:29.967
"to just a few notes.

00:18:29.967 --> 00:18:32.352
"Instead, we have held
that a four-note sequence

00:18:32.352 --> 00:18:35.467
"in the music field is
not the copyrightable

00:18:35.467 --> 00:18:36.870
"expression in a song."

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:38.850
And in Led Zeppelin, in order to prove

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:40.730
copyright infringement,
the two works at issue

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:42.830
would need to virtually identical.

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:44.870
The court found that "Stairway
to Heaven" and "Spirit"

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:46.180
were not virtually identical,

00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:47.937
just as the court did in Katy Perry.

00:18:47.937 --> 00:18:50.640
Now, the Led Zeppelin
case had mainly to do

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:53.190
with whether a defendant's
access to a song can be used

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:55.460
to prove copyright
infringement or plagiarism.

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:57.260
The 9th Circuit actually
reversed a precedent

00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:00.450
called the Inverse Ratio Rule
that had lasted for 45 years

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:02.473
and this is an issue I
have actually litigated

00:19:02.473 --> 00:19:05.280
and that particular rule deserved to die.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:08.670
See, the Inverse Ratio Rule
lent that the more access

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:11.660
that was shown between the
alleged copyright infringer

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:14.210
and the original copyrighted work,

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:16.520
the less substantial
similarity was required

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:18.090
to establish infringement.

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:20.101
That enabled plaintiffs to allege copying

00:19:20.101 --> 00:19:22.080
if they could show that the defendant

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:23.670
had likely heard the song.

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.600
And between Led Zeppelin and Taurus,

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:28.010
well they toured together in the 60s,

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:29.450
so there was almost no doubt

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:31.710
that Jimmy Page had heard Taurus' song.

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:34.010
But the thing is, that
probably shouldn't matter.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:36.780
Just because someone might
have heard or had access

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:39.243
to a song or other
creative work, doesn't mean

00:19:39.243 --> 00:19:42.880
that we should change the
standard required to show

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:44.429
that two things are similar.

00:19:44.429 --> 00:19:47.660
The more access doesn't
mean the less similarity

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:48.620
that you have to prove.

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:50.838
That unfairly burdens songs

00:19:50.838 --> 00:19:53.440
that simply got a lot of radio airtime.

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:55.750
The music industry has actually
been critical of this rule

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:58.370
for decades, especially since
it was not used by courts

00:19:58.370 --> 00:19:59.670
outside of the 9th Circuit.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:01.590
One of the reasons the music
industry hated this rule

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:04.120
was because access was
so much easier to prove

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:05.700
in the age of digital streaming.

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.320
Everyone pretty much has
access to any other song,

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:11.170
and in fact, Adam Neely did another video

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:13.544
talking about a group of
people who tried to copyright

00:20:13.544 --> 00:20:17.660
every conceivable melody
that could possibly exist

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:20.590
in the world, and they
sort of recognized though

00:20:20.590 --> 00:20:22.890
that not everyone has
access to this hard drive

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:26.580
of songs that they created,
so it might not actually solve

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:28.180
the issue that they're dealing with.

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:29.430
Definitely check out that video.

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:32.140
But after the Led Zeppelin
decision, to the extent

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:34.260
that the Inverse Ratio Rule ever existed,

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:35.960
which is sort of an open question,

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:37.300
at least in the 9th Circuit,

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:39.480
that rule has been completely abolished.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.686
And as in the Led Zeppelin
case with Katy Perry,

00:20:41.686 --> 00:20:45.420
when you filter out the
unprotected elements of the songs

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:47.390
at issue, there just isn't much left,

00:20:47.390 --> 00:20:49.120
and there certainly
isn't enough to justify

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:50.180
copyright infringement.

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:52.440
Now, those astute Legal Eagles out there

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:53.497
are probably wondering,

00:20:53.497 --> 00:20:55.877
"How come this case turned out different

00:20:55.877 --> 00:20:59.180
"than the Marvin Gaye
'Blurred Lines' case?"

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:03.340
Well, that is a really interesting
quirk of procedural law

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:06.463
because Katy Perry also
lost a jury verdict,

00:21:06.463 --> 00:21:08.320
but then was able to overturn it.

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:11.420
What happened with Robin
Thicke and "Blurred Lines"?

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.500
Well now, it's of course
possible that Katy Perry

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:16.050
may in fact lose her case
on appeal, but I think

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:17.510
that's probably really unlikely.

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:19.200
See, copyright issues are complicated

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:21.940
and they are often decided as
a matter of law by the court.

00:21:21.940 --> 00:21:23.990
Factual disputes, such as who was lying

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:26.880
and who was telling the
truth, are decided by a jury.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:30.060
But issues of law can
be decided by a judge

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:32.730
and generally, if you
can, you want you case

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:36.450
to be decided by a judge quickly,
before it gets to a jury,

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:38.220
because a jury can do anything.

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:39.053
Who knows?

00:21:39.053 --> 00:21:42.110
Now, in certain cases, you
can ask the judge to decide

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:45.480
in your favor as a matter of
law before it gets to trial.

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:48.240
Here, Katy Perry's lawyers
made a motion for judgment

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:50.930
as a matter of law before
it got to the jury.

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:53.900
The judge denied the motion,
and the case went to trial,

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:55.940
where the jury found it in favor of Flame

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:57.870
and awarded him 2.8 million dollars.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:01.450
But under the Federal Rules
of Civil Procedure, Rule 50,

00:22:01.450 --> 00:22:04.190
if you made a motion before the trial,

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:07.090
then you can make a
renewed motion afterwards,

00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:09.160
but you have to make that initial motion,

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:10.170
and that's what happened here.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:11.912
Katy Perry made the motion, it was denied.

00:22:11.912 --> 00:22:14.980
The Led Zeppelin case
came out in the interim,

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.990
they refiled after the jury
verdict, and then the judge,

00:22:17.990 --> 00:22:19.347
looking at the Led Zeppelin case said,

00:22:19.347 --> 00:22:22.990
"Okay this is on point", and
decided in Katy Perry's favor

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:26.030
and basically overturned
the decision of the jury.

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:28.677
Court basically said, "No
reasonable jury could have found

00:22:28.677 --> 00:22:31.150
"in favor of the
plaintiff", which is funny

00:22:31.150 --> 00:22:33.840
because all 12 jurors did find
in favor of the plaintiff,

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:35.580
but that's neither here nor there.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:38.550
Now, if a party fails
to make a Rule 50 motion

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:40.830
at the close of evidence,
that party waives its right

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:42.810
to challenge the
sufficiency of the evidence

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:44.030
of the verdict later on.

00:22:44.030 --> 00:22:47.470
This can be a very serious
mistake and to prove that,

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:49.410
all you have to do is ask Robin Thicke

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:52.820
and Pharrell's lawyers,
because they did not make

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:55.990
a Rule 50 motion for
judgment as a matter of law.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:58.470
And so, after the jury found against them

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:01.160
for millions of dollars, they weren't able

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:03.150
to make a renewed motion afterwards,

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:05.547
and it's entirely possible
that had they done that,

00:23:05.547 --> 00:23:07.390
the court would have sided with them

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:09.900
because a lot of people, a
lot of musicologists believe

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:13.700
that the "Blurred Lines"
lawsuit was incorrectly decided.

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:15.780
So the bottom line is that copyright law

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:17.966
is complicated and it's hard.

00:23:17.966 --> 00:23:21.040
Both the Led Zeppelin
and Katy Perry decisions

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:22.668
are examples of courts pushing back

00:23:22.668 --> 00:23:25.620
against the "Blurred
Lines" verdict, and I think

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:27.877
it's gonna be a lot harder to
prove copyright infringement

00:23:27.877 --> 00:23:30.820
in the 9th Circuit, for better or worse.

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:34.020
People borrow from predecessors
works and create new ones,

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.260
especially in the music
world, and we all share

00:23:36.260 --> 00:23:37.633
this common vocabulary.

00:23:37.633 --> 00:23:38.643
But at the end of the day,

00:23:38.643 --> 00:23:42.240
not all copying is copyright infringement,

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:45.125
just like not everything
that glitters is gold.

00:23:45.125 --> 00:23:47.664
- Does anybody remember laughter?

